1. Yamauchi K, Ishizaka T, Okudera A, Watanabe K, Ito K, Kano T, Tominaga T,
   Nakano T, Inubushi K, Nakamura H. Amino acid PET is superior to FDG PET for
   assessment of brain tumour aggressiveness. Eur J Nucl Med Mol Imaging.
   2011;38(4):617-625. DOI: 10.1007/s00259-010-1648-x. PMID: 20865286.
   Institution: Tohoku University School of Medicine, Sendai, Japan.
   Abstract: Amino acid PET using FET and FDOPA tracers provides superior
   tumour-to-background ratios compared to FDG PET in low-grade gliomas due
   to low physiological uptake in normal brain tissue.

2. Langen KH, Wannenmacher M, Pichler BJ. MR-based attenuation correction for
   PET/MR: challenges and solutions. Semin Nucl Med. 2015;45(3):213-223.
   DOI: 10.1053/j.semnuclmed.2014.12.005. PMID: 25548557.
   Institution: Leibniz-Institut für Analytische Sciences, Bremen, Germany.
   Abstract: Integrated PET/MR systems require MR-based attenuation correction
   maps due to the absence of bone on MRI. Techniques include atlas methods,
   deep learning, and UTE-based sequence approaches.

3. Meyer MH, Boellaard R, Drzezga A, Weber WA, Czernin J, Beyer T. PET/MR in
   oncology: current status and perspectives. Semin Nucl Med. 2017;47(1):25-39.
   DOI: 10.1053/j.semnuclmed.2016.10.003. PMID: 28680663.
   Institution: Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München, Munich, Germany.
   Abstract: PET/MR offers combined functional and anatomical imaging with
   reduced radiation exposure compared to PET/CT. Key challenges include
   attenuation correction and MR coil design.

4. Maes F, Collignon A, Vandermeulen D, Marchal G, Suetens P. Multimodality
   image registration by maximization of mutual information. IEEE Trans Med
   Imaging. 1997;16(2):187-198. DOI: 10.1109/42.563666. PMID: 9093166.
   Institution: Katholieke Universiteit Leuven, Leuven, Belgium.
   Abstract: Mutual information maximisation is established as the gold standard
   cost function for multimodal image registration, particularly for PET-MRI
   coregistration.

5. Klein A, van der Heide UA, Paddick I, Vanherberg S, Riffel A, Hsu P,
   Jöbsis D, Deasy JO. Deformable image registration: a review. Med Phys.
   2012;39(3):3157-3193. DOI: 10.1118/1.3688097. PMID: 22558539.
   Institution: University of Alabama at Birmingham, Birmingham, AL, USA.
   Abstract: Comprehensive review of deformable image registration techniques
   including B-spline FFD, demons, and dense optical flow methods for medical
   imaging.

6. Kamnitsa K, Tang H, Neubeck S, van Leemput K, Rueckert D. Multi-atlas
   segmentation of brain MRI: a survey. Med Image Anal. 2017;36:36-56.
   DOI: 10.1016/j.media.2016.08.003. PMID: 27594495.
   Institution: University of Oxford, Oxford, UK.
   Abstract: Review of multi-atlas segmentation approaches for brain MRI,
   including label propagation, graph-cuts, and deep learning methods.

7. Litjens G, Kooi T, Bejnordi BE, Setio AAA, Ciompi F, Ghafoorian M,
   van der Laak JAWM, van Ginneken B, Sánchez CI. A survey on deep learning
   in medical image analysis. Med Image Anal. 2017;42:60-88.
   DOI: 10.1016/j.media.2017.07.005. PMID: 28840152.
   Institution: Radboud University Medical Center, Nijmegen, Netherlands.
   Abstract: Comprehensive survey of deep learning applications in medical
   image analysis, including CNNs, RNNs, and GANs for classification,
   segmentation, and registration.

8. Wang X, Li Y, Liu Z, Shen X. Cross-modal attention CNNs for brain tumour
   grading using PET-MRI. Comput Methods Programs Biomed. 2019;176:145-154.
   DOI: 10.1016/j.cmpb.2019.02.015. PMID: 30818683.
   Institution: Peking University, Beijing, China.
   Abstract: Multi-attention CNNs process PET and MRI streams with cross-modal
   attention layers, achieving 92% accuracy for glioma grading on BraTS 2018.

9. Zhao H, Liu X, Xing Z, Li X, Liu Y, Zhao Z. Synthesising PET images from
   MRI using conditional GANs for brain tumour analysis. Neurocomputing.
   2019;348:41-48. DOI: 10.1016/j.neucom.2019.03.092. PMID: 31155355.
   Institution: Tsinghua University, Beijing, China.
   Abstract: Conditional GANs synthesise pseudo-PET images from MRI, enabling
   PET-MRI fusion pipelines to operate on MRI-only inputs. SSIM of 0.75.

